WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:05.130
Whether it's a single shot or a self-contained
narrative, a great opening sequence is the

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:08.860
most important part of a movie — the one
that convinces your audience to stay glued

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:10.929
to the screen until the end credits roll.

00:00:10.929 --> 00:00:15.539
And while the films on this list span different
genres, time periods, and techniques, all

00:00:15.539 --> 00:00:19.990
of them go to show just how incredible a movie's
first few minutes can be.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:21.390
The Godfather

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:25.930
Starting a movie with a monologue is a common
filmmaking device, but The Godfather does

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:27.120
it better than any other.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.800
"I believe in America.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.200
America has made my fortune."

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:37.170
From the moment Bonasera speaks, the scene
sucks viewers into a story about the immigrant

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.969
experience — but not the kind with a happy
ending.

00:00:39.969 --> 00:00:44.649
The American legal system has failed Bonasera,
setting free the men who beat his daughter

00:00:44.649 --> 00:00:46.339
within an inch of her life.

00:00:46.339 --> 00:00:47.339
His only hope?

00:00:47.339 --> 00:00:50.160
Don Vito Corleone, played by Marlon Brando.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.170
By the time this scene concludes, we know
that Don Corleone is a powerful criminal,

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:57.850
but also the man who people in his community
look to for justice when they have no one

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:02.319
else… and we definitely know better than
to change the channel on him.

00:01:02.319 --> 00:01:03.829
Trainspotting

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:08.000
The opening bars of Iggy Pop's song "Lust
for Life" will be forever associated with

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.850
this gripping sequence from Danny Boyle's
breakthrough film, which opens with a three-part

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:13.850
montage.

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:17.640
First, Renton — played by Ewan McGregor
— and Sick Boy — played by Jonny Lee Miller

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.840
— run through the streets after a theft.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.260
Then, they shoot up heroin in a run down apartment.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.300
And finally, of course, they play soccer---
Er, I mean football.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:26.300
Football, yeah.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.320
Ugh, saying 'soccer' felt so dirty.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.040
The film's other characters are introduced
along the way, all while McGregor gives a

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:36.690
monologue about choosing life — or rather,
choosing heroin instead of life — that puts

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:41.360
you in just the right mood for a frenetic,
edgy, and darkly comic story about the horrors

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.060
of addiction.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:44.260
Halloween

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:49.580
John Carpenter's 1978 horror classic heralded
the dawn of the slasher genre that would dominate

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:52.641
the 1980s — and it opens with a serious
scare.

00:01:52.641 --> 00:01:56.990
The first scene is shot from the point of
view of a killer, spying on some amorous teens

00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:01.330
before stalking and murdering one of them
in her bedroom, a brutal event we see through

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:04.400
the eyeholes of a mask that the unseen killer
is wearing.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.430
But it's not until the end of the sequence
that the killer's face is revealed.

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:08.869
The shocking truth?

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:13.000
You've just witnessed a murder committed by
a six-year-old boy — and learned the origin

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:18.250
story for iconic serial killer Michael Myers,
who's still terrorizing victims nearly forty

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:20.080
years later.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.580
Rear Window
Master of suspense Alfred Hitchcock knows

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:27.819
exactly how to set a scene, and he does so
in this case by showing us the world through

00:02:27.819 --> 00:02:30.730
the eyes of his hero… who's a bit of a peeping
tom.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:34.830
There's not a word of dialogue in this sequence,
but you know exactly what's going on: this

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:39.340
sweaty photographer with a broken leg and
a wall full of action-packed photographs is

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:43.660
trapped inside his apartment and starved for
adventure — which is hard to come by when

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:47.910
your sole source of entertainment is spying
on your neighbors, unless, oh, say, you happen

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.140
to spot one of them dragging a body around.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:58.680
Dazed and Confused

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:03.190
The opening sequence of Richard Linklater's
iconic stoner film drops us straight into

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:10.209
1976, as an orange 1970 Pontiac GTO circles
a parking lot and Aerosmith's "Sweet Emotion"

00:03:10.209 --> 00:03:11.290
plays in the background.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:15.550
This is where we first meet many of the film's
characters, who are at school, but also don't

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:19.540
seem to be taking their studies especially
seriously — which all makes sense when this

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:23.100
title card reveals this it's the last day
before summer break.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.709
No matter what era you're watching it in,
Dazed and Confused has you fully immersed

00:03:26.709 --> 00:03:30.080
in its world in fewer than three minutes.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:31.080
Star Wars: A New Hope

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:36.340
Yes, we all know how this movie begins — but
try to imagine watching its opening sequence

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:41.760
in 1977, when it was a mind-blowing sci-fi
setup like nothing anyone had ever seen.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:43.459
That dramatic text crawl!

00:03:43.459 --> 00:03:44.800
The alien planet!

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.550
A high speed pursuit through outer space featuring
two starships blasting each other with lasers!

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:53.300
But this wasn't just a way of showing off
Star Wars' groundbreaking special effects;

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:56.970
the mismatch in size between these two ships
makes it obvious that the first one is the

00:03:56.970 --> 00:04:01.640
underdog, which cleverly sets you up to root
for it even before you find out that the greatest

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:05.920
droids in the universe and a very important
princess are on board.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:07.700
Saturday Night Fever

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:12.220
Look at cool dude John Travolta, the physical
embodiment of all things disco.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:16.820
He's a woman's man, no time to talk — but
he does have time to shop, buy pizza, and

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:20.930
hit on a girl on his way to the paint store…
where an annoyed customer is waiting impatiently

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:21.930
for his return.

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:25.840
That's when we, the audience, realize that
the moments of joy he found in those first

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.820
few minutes were stolen and fleeting, a distraction
from his mundane blue collar life.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:33.870
Which is, of course, what this movie is all
about.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.449
Hustle and Flow

00:04:35.449 --> 00:04:38.120
First of all, let's get one thing straight.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.639
"See, man ain't like a dog."

00:04:40.639 --> 00:04:45.190
That's the opening line to an epic monologue
by Terence Howard, who has a lot to say about

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:47.840
the burden of consciousness on the minds of
men.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:52.630
But there's a twist to this philosophy lesson:
its audience of one is actually a sex worker

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:57.210
played by Taryn Manning, who's getting a lecture
from her pimp on why she should keep sleeping

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:58.460
with men for money.

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:02.470
It's a game-changing moment that sets the
stage for everything that comes next — and

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:05.470
a genius way to get the audience hooked.

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:06.790
The Dark Knight

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:10.900
This opening scene of Christopher Nolan's
most-beloved Batman flick is a mini-movie

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:15.690
in its own right: an exciting five-minute
short film about a bunch of criminals in creepy

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:17.960
clown masks pulling a bank heist.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:22.570
After this anonymous man gets picked up by
his gang, a complex robbery goes down — along

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:26.230
with some important scene-setting exposition
about the man who planned it.

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:28.320
"So why do they call him the joker?"

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:29.620
"I heard he wears makeup."

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:30.620
"Makeup?"

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:31.620
"Yeah, to scare people.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:32.620
You know, war paint."

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:35.900
As each man finishes his part of the job,
he's killed by his cohorts, until there's

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:39.100
only one left — and when he takes off his
clown mask?

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.100
Gah!

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:41.110
Nobody saw that coming!

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:45.080
To recap: five minutes into The Dark Knight
and you've already gotten an amazing action

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:49.840
sequence, an exciting twist, and a perfect
introduction to Batman's greatest enemy.

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:51.919
Now that's how you start a movie.

00:05:51.919 --> 00:05:53.020
Thanks for watching!

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:55.720
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:57.930
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

